## Findings

### Critical — The Phase 1 verify harness is not runnable and never records the owned peer identities

`stable_peers` is called when establishing `baseline_peers` but is no longer defined, so `set -Eeuo pipefail` exits before media certification. If that is fixed, the baseline checkpoint expands uninitialized `oc_peer_pid` and `pi_peer_pid` variables under `set -u`. Moreover, `capture_owned_peer` is never called and neither peer PID is ever assigned, so the harness cannot identify the two owned socket rows or prove those specific rows disappear after normal exits.

Initialize the peer identity state safely, capture exactly one owned row after each host launch, persist its PID/row with the corresponding process-group evidence, and use those recorded identities for post-exit and final absence checks.

### Critical — Exact unrelated-peer equality remains an executable final gate

Although most prose now correctly treats unrelated peers as context, the final harness still runs:

```sh
test "$(stable_peers)" = "$baseline_peers"
```

The whole-run definition of done also still says “Final peers equal baseline” and requires unrelated peers to be unchanged. This directly contradicts the dispatch and can reject a valid run when an unrelated client exits naturally. Replace the final equality with a captured final inventory plus absence checks limited to both recorded owned peer identities/process groups, and update the definition of done accordingly.

### High — The harness does not directly prove that the controlled source is Music.app

The dispatch requires a directly proven controllable Music.app source. `media_snapshot` discards `bundleIdentifier` and `processIdentifier`, while the interactive `source_label` is only an operator description. Consequently, any app publishing title/artist/playing metadata can satisfy the current checks.

Require and retain machine-verifiable Music.app identity (for example, `bundleIdentifier == "com.apple.Music"`, with its process identity recorded) in the canonical source evidence and verify it remains the same across direct controls, host controls, and restoration.
